## Scope

The MIT license above covers this repository EXCEPT for the vendored third-party
code listed below.

### PiFmRds — GPLv3

`PiFmRds/` is vendored from https://github.com/ChristopheJacquet/PiFmRds and is
licensed under the GNU General Public License v3. Its own terms are in
`PiFmRds/LICENSE` and they govern that directory. Nothing here relicenses it.

This matters in practice, not just on paper: the C sources in `PiFmRds/src/` are
served to devices over HTTP by `App\Http\Controllers\PiSetupController` and compiled
there, so running this application distributes GPLv3 code. Anyone who receives it is
entitled to the source under GPLv3 terms, which this repository provides verbatim.

### Two MIT files inside that directory

`PiFmRds/src/pi_daemon.py` and `PiFmRds/src/wifi_apply.sh` are original work of this
project and are MIT, like the rest of the application. They sit in that directory
because the installer places the whole payload in one place on the device, not
because they derive from PiFmRds. The daemon launches the `pi_fm_rds` binary as a
separate process and talks to it over its control pipe; it links no GPL code and
includes no GPL source.

If you would rather not rely on that distinction, moving those two files to a
directory of their own removes the question entirely.
